1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:24,400
 As you get more users, your database grows and your tables get bigger.

2
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:28,000
 Now it might take a little bit longer for your app to load and you're now having performance

3
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:29,120
 issues.

4
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:32,480
 One thing that you can always use to fix this is a database index.

5
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:37,180
 In this video, we'll go over what your database does when there is no index, what an index

6
00:00:37,180 --> 00:00:41,880
 actually is, how it helps find things so fast and helps performance, and the different types

7
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:46,640
 of indexes, the mistakes that people make when they use database indexes.

8
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:50,280
 So understand why a database index matters, especially for performance.

9
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:54,040
 You first have to understand what your database does without an index.

10
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:59,160
 Here we have a bookstores and the bookstable in our database has a million rows.

11
00:00:59,160 --> 00:01:03,120
 We have a million books in there and we run this query.

12
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:07,380
 Without an index, the database has no idea where Auto42's books are.

13
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:09,120
 So it does the only thing it can do.

14
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:14,360
 It starts as the first row and it checks every single row, all one million of them, and that

15
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:16,840
 is called a full table scan.

16
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:19,840
 And in many cases, it can be very problematic.

17
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:23,920
 When your table has maybe 10, 100 rows, maybe it wouldn't be a problem.

18
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:28,160
 Maybe you wouldn't notice, but at a million rows, then it's a problem.

19
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:29,800
 What is a database index?

20
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:32,840
 And how does it solve this problem of speed and performance?

21
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:36,200
 The easiest way to think about it is the index at the back of a book.

22
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:39,320
 If you want to find every page that mentions a particular word, you don't read the old

23
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:40,320
 book from the beginning.

24
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:44,520
 You go to the index at the back, it's sorted alphabetically, you find the word and it tells

25
00:01:44,520 --> 00:01:45,760
 you the page number.

26
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:48,400
 A database index works the same way.

27
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:53,560
 It's a separate structure that starts the value from a column in sorted order.

28
00:01:53,560 --> 00:01:57,120
 And for each value, it keeps a pointer to where that row actually lives.

29
00:01:57,120 --> 00:02:02,040
 And it only stores the column and the pointer, so it's way much more smaller than the table

30
00:02:02,040 --> 00:02:03,040
 itself.

31
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:06,320
 Now, what mechanism does a database index use to find things so fast?

32
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:10,160
 I read an article on this and I love the way the writer explained it and I'm going to use

33
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:11,160
 the example.

34
00:02:11,160 --> 00:02:13,000
 I will link the article in the description box as well.

35
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,160
 Say you have an index on the email column and you're looking for bob@email.com.

36
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:22,280
 The database opens the index in the middle and maybe sees dave@email.com.

37
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:23,520
 Bob comes before dave.

38
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:25,480
 So it moves to the left.

39
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:28,680
 Now it sees Alice@email.com.

40
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:31,120
 Bob comes after Alice, so it moves to the right.

41
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:35,840
 And it keeps moving strategically like this until it finds bob@email.com in the index.

42
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:40,760
 Bob's entry in the index has a pointer to where Bob's row is in the table.

43
00:02:40,760 --> 00:02:44,800
 So with this strategy, even with a million rows, you can get to the row you're looking

44
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:47,920
 for in about 20 steps or so.

45
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:52,080
 Without an index, you will do up to a million check in the worst case scenario.

46
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:54,680
 Without an index, you will do way less than that.

47
00:02:54,680 --> 00:02:58,800
 Most databases store their indexes in a structure called a B3.

48
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:03,000
 And a B3 works the way that the example from the article showed.

49
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:09,600
 So even with a table with millions of rows, you usually only need a few levels to get

50
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:10,920
 to the right place.

51
00:03:10,920 --> 00:03:14,880
 Adding a database query will literally up your query optimization goal.

52
00:03:14,880 --> 00:03:16,320
 And it's kind of a one-liner.

53
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:17,720
 In SKA, it looks something like this.

54
00:03:17,720 --> 00:03:21,760
 If you're using Laravel, you're using Laravel for the past one year, for example, you simply

55
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:23,560
 add it in your migration like this.

56
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:28,360
 Now, when you search books by author, the database goes to the index first, finds author

57
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:31,040
 42, and goes straight to those rows, rows.

58
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:34,400
 Once you've added your database index, you can confirm whether your query is actually

59
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:35,400
 using it or not.

60
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:36,400
 You can ask the database.

61
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:39,600
 Just put the word "splain" in front of your query.

62
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:42,520
 In Postgres SQL, you will see "quen scan."

63
00:03:42,520 --> 00:03:46,600
 It means that the database is reading every single row.

64
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:49,800
 And if you see "index scan," it means that the database is using your index.

65
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:52,880
 "S scan," "sequential scan," "sex scan."

66
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:58,000
 In MySQL, the results will come back in a small table, so there are two columns to look

67
00:03:58,000 --> 00:03:59,000
 at.

68
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,400
 If it says "all," that means it's doing a full table scan.

69
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:04,480
 If it says something like "ref or range," then it's using an index.

70
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:08,280
 So if a query is slow and you first want to confirm what is going on, you can use-- and

71
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:11,960
 that confirms to you, is this query doing a full scan or is it using an index?

72
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:13,960
 There are a few types of database index.

73
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:18,560
 You hear people talk about B3, Ash, Bitmap, and many, many like that.

74
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:22,840
 I'm just only going to focus on three in this video.

75
00:04:22,840 --> 00:04:25,280
 The first one is B3, B3.

76
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:29,600
 The B3 is the default one, so when you create an index without saying what type it is, this

77
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:31,560
 is the one that you get.

78
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:32,560
 So it's very common.

79
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:36,000
 It can find exact matches, like the user with this email.

80
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,940
 It can find ranges, like books priced between 10 and 15.

81
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:43,000
 It can sort, like showing the newest order first.

82
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,840
 And it can find values by how they start, like every book title that starts with Ari.

83
00:04:46,840 --> 00:04:48,800
 And that works because the index is sorted.

84
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:51,880
 So all the titles that start with Ari sit next to each other.

85
00:04:51,880 --> 00:04:56,000
 The second commonly used type of database index is the unique index.

86
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,880
 It makes sure that no two rows have the same value.

87
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:01,480
 For example, no two user can sign up with the same email.

88
00:05:01,480 --> 00:05:04,160
 Your primary query already has one.

89
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:06,960
 And the third one-- this one kind of always confuses me.

90
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:11,120
 The third one is the composites index, which is an index on more than one column.

91
00:05:11,120 --> 00:05:14,960
 So say your index, last name, and first name together in that order.

92
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:18,640
 It helps you when you search by last name, and it helps you when you search by last name

93
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:19,640
 and first name.

94
00:05:19,640 --> 00:05:22,040
 But when you search by only first name, it usually doesn't help.

95
00:05:22,040 --> 00:05:25,320
 A little bit of a reason-- let's use the example with phone books to understand it.

96
00:05:25,320 --> 00:05:27,560
 Think of a phone book sorted by surname.

97
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:29,680
 All the adderia means are together.

98
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:32,160
 And for that, all the ocafos are together.

99
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:36,160
 And inside each surname, it is sorted by first name.

100
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:40,040
 So in the ocafos, adda ocafo will come before chidi ocafo.

101
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:42,040
 So if I'm looking for adda ocafo, it's easy.

102
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:45,520
 I go to the ocafo, I find ocafo, and then I find adda.

103
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:48,720
 But what if I want everyone whose first name is adda?

104
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:53,400
 There's probably adda adeyemi in the front, and adda ocafo in the middle, and adda williams

105
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:54,400
 near the end.

106
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:59,140
 They are spread across the old phone book because the book is sorted by surname first.

107
00:05:59,140 --> 00:06:03,600
 So nothing groups the addas together, and I would have to read every page to find them.

108
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:07,880
 So that's exactly what happens with the composites index on last name, first name.

109
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:15,640
 So the tip is to put the column you search by most often first.

110
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:19,120
 And for the rest, there are different kinds of searches.

111
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:23,600
 For the rest of the database index types, we have the hash index, which is for exact

112
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:26,600
 matches only.

113
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:29,900
 Like looking up someone's login session by its exact token.

114
00:06:29,900 --> 00:06:33,840
 It cannot do range or sorting, so most people just use b3.

115
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:37,680
 A bitmap is for columns with only a few values, like a status column.

116
00:06:37,680 --> 00:06:39,240
 It's mostly active or inactive or banned.

117
00:06:39,240 --> 00:06:42,960
 You mostly see it in databases that are built for reports, yeah?

118
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:46,040
 And then we have spatial index, that one is mostly for location data.

119
00:06:46,040 --> 00:06:50,920
 Like when you search restaurant name, a coffee shop name, or when a write app looks for drivers

120
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:52,160
 close to you.

121
00:06:52,160 --> 00:06:55,300
 So it's groups, places that are near each other on the app.

122
00:06:55,300 --> 00:06:56,300
 So on the map.

123
00:06:56,300 --> 00:07:01,360
 So the app only checks places around you instead of every coffee shop or every restaurant in

124
00:07:01,360 --> 00:07:02,360
 the world.

125
00:07:02,360 --> 00:07:04,360
 And then we have the full test index as well.

126
00:07:04,360 --> 00:07:05,960
 It is for searching inside text.

127
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:10,240
 For example, when you search an online store for wireless headphones, it finds every product

128
00:07:10,240 --> 00:07:12,960
 description with those words in it, right?

129
00:07:12,960 --> 00:07:16,680
 So it works by indexing each word in the test instead of the whole value.

130
00:07:16,680 --> 00:07:20,200
 And there's also covering index and there's partial index.

131
00:07:20,200 --> 00:07:23,800
 You also hear about clustered and non-clustered indexes.

132
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:26,200
 And the difference between those is very simple.

133
00:07:26,200 --> 00:07:30,640
 The clustered index decides the order the table's rules are actually stored.

134
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:32,240
 Think of this as a phone book again.

135
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:33,840
 The data itself is sorted by Sony.

136
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:35,640
 So once you found the name, you found everything.

137
00:07:35,640 --> 00:07:39,440
 A table can only have one clustered index because the rules can only be stored in one

138
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:40,440
 order.

139
00:07:40,440 --> 00:07:44,800
 A non-clustered index is a separate structure that points back to the rule.

140
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:46,920
 Think of that as the index at the back of a book.

141
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:48,740
 It tells you where to go and go there.

142
00:07:48,740 --> 00:07:52,680
 So a table can have lots of non-clustered indexes.

143
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:55,640
 So this is the difference between clustered and non-clustered indexes.

144
00:07:55,640 --> 00:08:01,160
 So now that begs the question, if database indexes makes queries so fast, why don't we

145
00:08:01,160 --> 00:08:04,160
 just index every column and be done with it?

146
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:05,160
 We cannot.

147
00:08:05,160 --> 00:08:06,760
 So every index has a cost.

148
00:08:06,760 --> 00:08:11,200
 Every time you insert, update, and delete a rule, the database also has to update every

149
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:12,320
 index on that table.

150
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:17,040
 So if your other table has six indexes, every new order means updating the old table and

151
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:18,440
 all six indexes.

152
00:08:18,440 --> 00:08:22,560
 So the more database indexes you have, the slower your writes get.

153
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:25,200
 And also, indexes also take up storage space.

154
00:08:25,200 --> 00:08:30,200
 And for a small table, the database will often skip your index and just count the table anyway

155
00:08:30,200 --> 00:08:33,680
 because that's faster for a small table if you have just like 10 rules.

156
00:08:33,680 --> 00:08:36,880
 It's faster to read them than to go through an index first.

157
00:08:36,880 --> 00:08:42,040
 So what you do is that you only index the columns that you actually search on or filter

158
00:08:42,040 --> 00:08:43,520
 on or join or sort by.

159
00:08:43,520 --> 00:08:44,760
 You don't index everything.

160
00:08:44,760 --> 00:08:48,320
 But because as good as it is, it can also get costly too.

161
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:52,080
 And there are several mistakes you can make that can stop your database index from working

162
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:53,080
 as well.

163
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:57,240
 You can have a database index and the database too wouldn't use it.

164
00:08:57,240 --> 00:08:59,580
 And these are the common mistakes that cause it.

165
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:02,000
 The first one is wrapping your colon in a function.

166
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:07,800
 For example, in this case, a normal index on email is sorted by the emails exactly as

167
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:09,120
 they were stored.

168
00:09:09,120 --> 00:09:12,800
 The lowercase versions are not in that budget.

169
00:09:12,800 --> 00:09:17,180
 So the database can use it here unless you create an index on the same expression.

170
00:09:17,180 --> 00:09:23,040
 The second is you can also be adding a wildcard at the start of a like.

171
00:09:23,040 --> 00:09:25,840
 The index is sorted by how each value starts.

172
00:09:25,840 --> 00:09:29,200
 And if you don't know how it starts, the index cannot help you find it.

173
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:34,620
 Another database index gotcha is that in MySQL, when you had a foreign key, the database

174
00:09:34,620 --> 00:09:40,160
 creates an index on that column for you automatically, if there isn't one already.

175
00:09:40,160 --> 00:09:42,340
 But in Postgres SQL, it doesn't.

176
00:09:42,340 --> 00:09:46,860
 So if you're on Postgres or you've moved from MySQL to Postgres, you have to check and confirm

177
00:09:46,860 --> 00:09:50,000
 that your foreign key columns actually have indexes.

178
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:53,860
 And it's one of the common reasons why it joins suddenly will get slow.

179
00:09:53,860 --> 00:09:57,160
 So again, to reiterate when to actually use a database index.

180
00:09:57,160 --> 00:10:03,860
 Use it when a column keeps showing up in your where, clauses, your joins or your order by.

181
00:10:03,860 --> 00:10:08,560
 So say you are sorting a million orders by date, you only show the first, and you only

182
00:10:08,560 --> 00:10:09,560
 show the first 10.

183
00:10:09,560 --> 00:10:15,760
 If there is no index, the database has to do, look at a million rows and sort them before

184
00:10:15,760 --> 00:10:17,160
 it can give you those 10.

185
00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:22,040
 But with an index, it read them in order and stops at the first, after the first 10.

186
00:10:22,040 --> 00:10:27,720
 So when you use explain and you realize that a query is doing a full table scan and it

187
00:10:27,720 --> 00:10:31,140
 is slow, then maybe you need an index there.

188
00:10:31,140 --> 00:10:35,460
 So if your query is slow, you probably should consider adding an index there.

189
00:10:35,460 --> 00:10:41,720
 And if it's doing a full scan, also when a table gets more read, like you read it a lot

190
00:10:41,720 --> 00:10:44,960
 more than you write it, that's also a good situation for a database index.

191
00:10:44,960 --> 00:10:48,840
 But you shouldn't do that when the table is small, or when the table is constantly reaching

192
00:10:48,840 --> 00:10:53,320
 to or really search, then you probably don't need a database index in that situation.

193
00:10:53,320 --> 00:10:54,920
 So that's it to its database indexes.

194
00:10:54,920 --> 00:10:55,120
 Bye.

